## 8. Content workflow

Text lives in files, never in components.

- `messages/*.json`: website text, grouped by page/section (`home.title`, `contact.hours`, `meta.homeTitle`). Current groups: `nav` (header links, mobile menu labels), `localeSwitcher`, `social` (link names for the Facebook/Instagram logos), `home` (`home.hero.title`, `home.hero.cta`), `menu`, `gallery`, `about`, `contact`. Brand names go in as ICU placeholders filled from `data/site.ts` (`"Dobrodošli u svijet {name}"` with `t("title", { name: site.fullName })`), never typed into the JSON.
- `data/menu/`: `index.ts` (the categories), one file of sections per category (`dorucak.ts`, `potazi.ts`, ... `nasi-proizvodi.ts`, `posni-meni.ts`; Slatka karta is still in the three files it was imported into, `pice.ts`, `dezerti.ts`, `sladoledi.ts`), `types.ts`. The client's main-menu export is kept in `data/menu/incoming/` (`menu.json` and its README with the known typos and missing numbers; its photos were deleted after the import, as they are in `public/images/menu/`), so texts can be fixed in the JSON and re-imported. (An old `data/menu/index.ts` shadowed this folder, since a file wins over a folder's `index.ts`; it was deleted on 2026-10-16.)
- `scripts/import-menu.ts` (`npm run menu:import -- <menu.json> <slug>=<category id>,... ... [--skip=<item id>,...]`) turns a client JSON menu (Montenegrin + English, photos with transparent backgrounds) into `data/menu/<slug>.ts` and copies the photos to `public/images/menu/<slug>/`. Each JSON category becomes a section of the given site category, with its id as the section id; one with `sections` of its own (Budi sam svoj šef) becomes those sections. It keeps the printed dish `number` (numbered dishes sorted by it) and the `noPork` badge; `--skip` leaves out dishes already in another category. The command for the main menu is in the script's header. It overwrites the file, so hand fixes there are lost on a re-import.
- `data/site.ts`: brand name, address, phone, hours, socials (same in every language; only the labels go in JSON)

Process:
1. The client's owner writes everything in **Montenegrin first** (`messages/me.json` and the `me` fields in `menu.ts`).
2. Until translations exist, copy `me.json` into `en/it/ru/tr.json` so the site builds.
3. When the Montenegrin content is final, translate into EN, IT, RU, TR. Keep keys identical and do not touch `{placeholders}` or HTML tags.
4. Run `npm run i18n:check`.
5. Native speakers proofread IT, RU, TR (food terms and allergen information especially).

**`data/menu/index.ts` format**: each category has optional `sections` (like "Posni sendviči"), each section a list of dishes. Dish and section texts are `L` objects in `menu.ts`, not in `messages/*.json`. Until a dish is translated, `untranslated("...")` puts the Montenegrin text in all 5 languages (`meEn(me, en)` when the client sent English too: the JSON menus), so searching for `untranslated(` lists what is left to translate (`npm run i18n:check` does not see `menu.ts`; the `L` type makes a missing language fail the build).
```ts
{
  id: 'posni-sendvici', // id of the section heading, unique on the page
  title: untranslated('Posni sendviči'),
  dishes: [
    {
      name: untranslated('Sendvič 1'),
      description: untranslated('Biljni sir, avokado sos, masline, pasulj, pomfrit'),
      price: 6,
      image: sendvic1, // static import from public/images/menu/<slug>/
    },
  ],
}
```
Prices are numbers in EUR, shown with `toFixed(2)` and the € sign. Dish photos go in `public/images/menu/<category slug>/<dish>.webp`.

