### Hero (`components/sections/hero.tsx`, home page)

- Full-bleed background photo, **a separate image per screen size** (art direction): `getImageProps()` from `next/image` for each image, combined in a `<picture>` with `<source media>` (Next's documented pattern). The browser downloads only the matching one.

  | Screen | Media query | File | Shape |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | Phone | default (`< 48rem`) | `public/images/hero/mobile-bg.jpg` (**client's photo**: a waiter serving a mille-feuille on a terracotta plate) | 2:3 portrait, 1333x2000 |
  | Tablet | `(min-width: 48rem)` (Tailwind `md`) | `public/images/hero/tablet.jpg` | 4:5, 1600x2000 |
  | Desktop | `(min-width: 64rem)` (Tailwind `lg`) | `public/images/hero/desktop.jpg` | wide, 2880x1639 |

  Why: on a phone the hero is tall and narrow, so a single landscape photo gets cropped and enlarged about 3.5x and looks soft. Each `<source>` has `sizes="100vw"`. The `<img>` has `fill` styles, `object-cover`, `loading="eager"` and `fetchPriority="high"`. Do not use `preload` here: it would preload the phone image on every device. There is no blur placeholder, because the section background is `bg-brand-cocoa`, so the area is dark while the photo loads. Decorative, so `alt=""`; the heading carries the meaning. Verified: phones (375–430px at 3x) download only `mobile-bg`, an 820px tablet only `tablet`, a 1440px desktop only `desktop`.
- To swap in real photos: replace the three files, keeping the names and shapes. Keep the subject in the middle of each crop, with calmer space at the bottom where the text sits.
- Height: `min-h-[calc(100svh-var(--header-bar)-4px)]`, so **header + hero = exactly one screen** on every device (`svh` avoids the jump when the mobile address bar shows/hides). It is a minimum: on short screens (landscape phones) it grows to fit the text.
- Readability: two overlays over the photo. `bg-brand-cocoa/50` darkens all of it (this also makes the header logo and links stand out), and a bottom-up gradient `from-brand-cocoa/80 via-brand-cocoa/20 to-transparent` darkens the text area more. Measured, cream heading vs. the photo behind it (worst = over the brightest spot): phone with the client's photo at least 5.9:1 (iPhone SE 375x667), 7.6:1 (390x844) and 8.4:1 (430x932); tablet placeholder 8.3:1; desktop placeholder 6.3:1; medians 12–15:1. Re-check whenever a photo changes. On phones the heading overlaps the lower part of the dessert in `mobile-bg.jpg`. That is readable, but the subject is partly behind the text.
- Content, bottom-left in `max-w-7xl`, with `pt-32` (on short screens it keeps the text clear of the hanging logo badge), and `animate-fade-in` as the page's one deliberate motion:
  - h1 `home.hero.title` with `{name}` = `site.fullName` ("Dobrodošli u svijet Hari's Creperie"), Lora `font-semibold`, cream, `text-4xl sm:text-5xl md:text-6xl`.
  - Link to `/meni` styled with `buttonVariants({ variant: "secondary" })` (peach, dark maroon text, 9.3:1), `px-4 py-2.5`, Lora semibold, arrow icon at the end, cream focus ring (the default maroon one is invisible on the photo).
- **Placeholder photos (tablet and desktop only):** both are crops of one stock photo, "a plate of pancakes with whipped cream and strawberries" by Michael Moloney (@mjmolo) on Unsplash (unsplash.com/photos/d8b4yr2kVFI, original 5000x2846), Unsplash License (free for commercial use, no attribution required). The tablet crop shows the whole crepe, and the desktop crop is the full frame. They are not the client's food: replace them with real photos of the place before launch.

